## Verbose: [OFF]
Set to [ON] to get running commentary during execution. When ON, the agent appends status lines to the Session Log after each sub-task and writes a Session Summary at the end of every run.

## Operating Rules

1. **One bundle per session.** Pick exactly one bundle from the Active Work Bundles below and complete ALL tasks within it. Do not start a second bundle until the current one is fully done.
2. **Project directory on first grab.** When an item is selected to work on, create (or verify) the project directory. Immediately update this TODO file with the folder path next to the task.
3. **Skill files live in the project directory.** All skill markdown files and associated scripts are saved inside the project's own directory, not in a global agent skills directory. Example: `SKILL.md` at the project root.
4. **Document hosts immediately.** If a host or VM is provisioned during work, log its name and address in the Session Log and in the project's skill file. This information is critical for future sessions.
5. **50-step hard cap.** Each run has approximately 50 steps (tool calls, reasoning turns) before context resets. Count your turns. Save progress to disk frequently — any discovery not written down by step 50 is lost.
6. **Resume from project files.** When picking up an item that already has a directory, read the existing skill file and project contents first to understand where you left off before continuing.
7. **Save early and often.** After each significant discovery or breakthrough, update the skill file immediately. Do not wait until the end of a session.
8. **Verbose mode.** If the `## Verbose` flag below is set to `[ON]`, provide a running commentary during execution. After each completed sub-task, append a brief status line to the Session Log with what was done, any output or errors seen, and what comes next.
9. **Session summary.** At the end of every run (whether successful or not), write a short paragraph under the Session Summary heading at the bottom of this file describing: which bundle was worked on, what was accomplished, what failed (if anything), and what to do next.
10. **Write unit tests for projects.** When working on a multi-session project (not a one-off), write unit tests for core logic that can be tested without network access. Save them as `tests/test_<name>.py`. Run them after any significant code change.
11. **Tests must pass before deploy.** `pytest tests/` must return exit code 0 with zero failures before any commit or deploy. No skipping "pre-existing" failures — fix them first.
12. **Run the full test suite on every change.** Running targeted tests instead of `pytest tests/` hides failures you didn't cause. Always run the full suite.
13. **Create a git repo for every project.** When creating a new project directory, immediately `git init && git add -A && git commit`. Commit after every meaningful change.
14. **No real mail, no employee addresses, no credentials, no live alert data in any commit.** Replay recordings are generated against `fixtures/synthetic/` only — a recording contains the prompt, and the prompt contains the message. See the data policy in `NOTES.md`. The Exchange scans full git history and rejects automatically.
15. **No private infrastructure detail in tracked files.** Internal hostnames, IP addresses, tunnel configuration, service paths and colleague names stay out of files destined for the public repository. Until the Bundle G split, they live in `NOTES.md`, which is tracked but not yet published. This file is written to be publishable as-is.

