**Bug 1, found while building it: a label set that silently did not cover a
corpus.** `DEFERRALS`, `ACCUSATIONS` and `HOSTILE_LABELS` were defined in both
`eval/run.py` and `eval/selective.py`, and had already drifted - the harness said
hostile meant `{threat, spam}` while the metrics also knew `{hostile, phishing,
malicious}`. Every case in `fixtures/adversarial/` is labelled `hostile`, so all 18
came back undecidable and selective risk was `None` on precisely the corpus where
the risk is most interesting. It did not look broken; it looked like an honest
"this corpus has no ground truth". Fixed by giving the label sets one
owner - `eval/selective.py`, imported by `eval/run.py` - by reporting
`undecidable` next to every rate, and by a test asserting it is zero on both
corpora. The lesson is general: when a classifier over a taxonomy matches
nothing, the absence of a match and the presence of no answer must be
distinguishable, and the safe direction to fail is to count what was not understood
rather than to fold it into the denominator.

**Bug 2, found by reading the number: a negative E-AURC.** The oracle first used
the textbook closed form `e0 + (1-e0)ln(1-e0)`, which assumes a selector can reach
full coverage by lowering its threshold. This one cannot: deferral is a decision
about evidence, not about confidence, so the adversarial curve stops at 11.1%
coverage. Comparing that curve against an oracle integrated to 1.0 gave a negative
excess - impossible, since the oracle is the minimum by construction. The oracle is
now computed over the curve's own coverage grid, like for like, and `excess_aurc`
returns an `inconsistent` marker instead of clamping a negative, so the next time
the comparison is wrong it announces itself. The lesson: a formula copied from a
paper carries that paper's assumptions, and a property test (`E-AURC >= 0` over
four corpus shapes) catches what a worked example cannot - a worked example only
shows you the number you expected.

**What the original scoping paragraph got wrong, corrected here rather than
repeated.** It claimed the deterministic layer "already reaches the right answer on
all 32 messages, so selective risk at full coverage is zero and the curve is flat".
True of the synthetic corpus, false of the adversarial one. Measured, both
configurations, from `eval/metrics.json`: synthetic 14 messages, 10 automated and
4 deferred, coverage **71.4%**, selective risk **0.0%**, AURC 0.0000, oracle 0.0000,
E-AURC 0.0000, 5 curve points, and every risk budget in the reported range
achievable at 71.4% coverage; adversarial 18 messages, 2 automated and 16 deferred,
coverage **11.1%**, selective risk **50.0%**, AURC **0.0417**, oracle **0.0139**,
E-AURC **0.0278**, 3 curve points, and **no automated-error budget in the reported
range achievable**. The single wrong automated decision on the hostile corpus is the
disclosed open finding `forged_auth_single_header`, cleared at the system's highest
confidence, 0.90 - which is why E-AURC is positive there and is the documented
"these are policy labels, not probabilities" caveat, now measured instead of merely
stated. The flat curve and the honest limit therefore survive where they belong - on
the synthetic corpus, where nothing is automated wrongly because there is nothing
contentious to automate - and README and `docs/PARITY.md` row 32 say it that way.

**The null result this bundle produced is the result.** Thirty-two messages we wrote
ourselves demonstrate the method and cannot evidence it. Selective prediction over
these corpora tells an operator how the machinery behaves, not how the system
performs; the numbers become evidence only against an external corpus, which is
Bundle N and is why M was written as depending on it rather than replacing it.

**A third defect, found while writing the tests for the section.** The
deferred-slice sentence printed "16 escalated by a model tier, 0 model-assisted" -
contradicting itself three words apart, because `Verdict.escalated` is set from the
disposition as well as from tier disagreement, and the deterministic-only
configuration has no reachable model at all. The measurement was right; the prose
invented a cause. What it taught: a rendered sentence is a claim, and the test that
catches it has to assert the configuration really contains no model before asserting
