- **Bundle P - Deduplicate repeated reports of the same message. SCOPED 2026-11-03,
  NOT STARTED.** Asked by the operator: if several people report the same message, are
  we re-running the full suite and any escalation on it each time, and are we using the
  repetition as a signal?

  **Current state, verified rather than assumed.** There is no deduplication. Grepping
  the package for `dedup`, `duplicate`, `seen_before`, `report_count` and
  `times_reported` finds nothing relevant. The `cases` table does not even store
  `message_id`, so there is presently no way to group re-reports at all. Re-running the
  same message is idempotent only *at the ledger write*, and only incidentally: the
  receipt id is derived from verdict content, so `record_receipt` returns the existing
  case instead of inserting a second one. The full pipeline still executes every time,
  including any escalation, and the fact that forty people reported it is thrown away.

  **Why this matters, in the operator's terms rather than as an optimisation.** In a real
  campaign the same message is reported dozens of times within an hour. Two consequences
  follow. Cost: every repeat pays for the whole chain again, and the expensive top rung
  is precisely the one that should not be paid twice for identical bytes. Signal: report
  volume is itself evidence, and the reference platform tracks it - PhishER's schema
  carries `matchedCount` on both `links` and `rules`, and `firstSeen`/`lastSeen` per
  link, which is how a single message becomes a visible campaign rather than a queue of
  unrelated cases.

  Scope:
  - [ ] P1: store `content_digest` (sha256 over the raw RFC 822 bytes) and `message_id`
        on the `cases` table, with an index on the digest. Note the two are different
        keys with different trust levels - the digest is a fact about the bytes we
        received, while `message_id` is an RFC 822 header that is routinely absent,
        duplicated or **forged**, so it must never be the primary dedup key. Group on
        the digest; record the header as a secondary, weaker association.
  - [ ] P2: a `reports` table, append-only and foreign-keyed to the case, holding
        `reported_at`, `reporter` and `source`. One row per report, so a case that
        forty people reported has forty rows and the first verdict is untouched. This
        fits the existing append-only invariant rather than working around it.
  - [ ] P3: short-circuit re-analysis. On a content digest already present, record the
        new report against the existing case and return the existing verdict without
        re-running the chain or re-escalating. **This must be opt-in and observable**,
        not silent: the operator has to be able to see that analysis was skipped and
        why, and there must be a way to force re-analysis, because a verdict computed
        against yesterday's reputation data may be stale today. Follow the existing
        rule that cached live state must be validated for freshness before being
        reported as current.
  - [ ] P4: surface `report_count`, `first_reported` and `last_reported` in the case
        record, the CLI `ledger show`, the web view and `agreement`.
  - [ ] P5: **report volume is a fact, not an accusation.** Forty reports of a message
        is a campaign indicator worth showing, and one report is not evidence of
        anything. It must not raise a disposition or contribute to a THREAT on its own,
        for the same reason a destination mismatch does not: legitimate mail gets
        over-reported constantly, and the synthetic corpus already contains a marketing
        message labelled `spam` precisely because somebody over-reported it. Record it,
        show it, let a human weigh it. If it ever blocks an automatic clearance, that is
        a policy change needing its own justification and its own test.
  - [ ] P6: the same digest must not be escalated twice. Escalation is the expensive
        rung and Bundle K's whole premise is reserving it for cases that earn it, so
        re-escalating identical bytes is the clearest possible waste of it.
  - [ ] P7: tests covering - two reports of the same bytes produce one case and two
        report rows; the machine verdict is byte-identical after the second report;
        analysis was genuinely skipped rather than merely re-recorded; a forced
        re-analysis works and says it re-ran; two *different* messages sharing a forged
        `message_id` do NOT collapse into one case; and report volume changes no
